# core.autocrlf is true in this clone, so anything git checks out gets CRLF
# unless told otherwise. A shell script with CRLF endings dies on its own
# shebang (`/bin/sh^M: bad interpreter`), silently, on a fresh clone — so the
# hooks are pinned to LF.
tools/hooks/* text eol=lf

# ⛔ The line above used to be followed by "Nothing else in the repo needs
# pinning: the Lua and the docs are read by tools that do not care." That was
# FALSE, and it is why this pin took three weeks to arrive: doccheck.py counts
# STATE.md's bytes RAW off the disk against a 9216-byte warn, so a CRLF
# checkout adds one byte per line and warns on content that never changed.
# Measured 2026-09-09: 9211 B LF over 105 lines = 9316 B as CRLF, i.e. 100
# bytes over the warn on a fresh clone, with 5 bytes of real headroom. A
# session that does not check the CR count evicts real kernel content to chase
# a checkout artefact — that already produced one wrong diagnosis (a session
# calling it a "transient"). Pinned so the cap measures the resource and not
# the checkout. ⇒ Any file a tool measures in BYTES belongs here.
docs/agent/STATE.md text eol=lf

# Append-only companions preserve this sweep's original mixed log line endings.
# Recorded SHA256 hashes describe these raw bytes; readable .log blobs normalize.
docs/archive/logs/stillneeded_*.log.raw -text
